language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ netasgn: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Consider a project assignment problem with a set of people and a set of projects. Each person has a certain number of available hours, and each project requires a certain number of hours. The cost per hour of work for a person on a project is given, and each person can contribute to a project up to a maximum limit of hours. Decide how many hours each person works on each project so that all the available hours of each person are assigned, the hours required by each project are met exactly, each limit is respected, and the total cost is minimised.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `netasgn.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/netasgn.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `netasgn_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py netasgn.json
$   savilerow model.eprime netasgn_1.param -run-solver

$ number of people, derived from the data: the length of supply (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given supply_dim1 : int
$ number of projects, derived from the data: the length of demand (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given demand_dim1 : int
$ supply[i]: the available hours of person i (indices 1-based: the data's index 0 is 1)
given supply : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ demand[j]: the hours that project j requires
given demand : matrix indexed by [int(1..demand_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ cost[i,j]: the cost per hour of work of person i on project j
given cost : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1), int(1..demand_dim1)] of int(0..)
$ limit[i,j]: the most hours person i can contribute to project j
given limit : matrix indexed by [int(1..supply_dim1), int(1..demand_dim1)] of int(0..)

letting people = supply_dim1
letting projects = demand_dim1
$ the total available hours: an upper bound on the hours assigned to any one pair
letting total_supply = sum i : int(1..people) . supply[i]

$ assign[i,j]: the hours that person i works on project j (indices 1-based)
find assign : matrix indexed by [int(1..people), int(1..projects)] of int(0..total_supply)
$ total_cost: the total cost of the hours assigned
find total_cost : int(0..total_supply * (sum i : int(1..people) . sum j : int(1..projects) . cost[i,j]))

minimising total_cost
such that
$ the hours assigned from each person equal that person's available hours
forAll i : int(1..people) . ((sum j : int(1..projects) . assign[i,j]) = supply[i]),
$ the hours assigned to each project equal the project's required hours
forAll j : int(1..projects) . ((sum i : int(1..people) . assign[i,j]) = demand[j]),
$ the hours assigned from a person to a project do not exceed the limit on that pair
forAll i : int(1..people) . forAll j : int(1..projects) . (assign[i,j] <= limit[i,j]),
$ the total cost is the cost per hour times the hours assigned, summed over all pairs
total_cost = (sum i : int(1..people) . sum j : int(1..projects) . cost[i,j] * assign[i,j])
